# Trackless Android SDK — AI Integration Rules

This package is `com.tracklesstelemetry:sdk-android`, a privacy-first Android analytics SDK. See GUIDE.md in this directory for the complete integration guide.

## Quick Reference

1. Install: add `com.tracklesstelemetry:sdk-android` (latest release) to your Gradle dependencies — see GUIDE.md for the snippet
2. Configure once in Application.onCreate with just an API key:
   ```kotlin
   import com.tracklesstelemetry.sdk.Trackless
   import com.tracklesstelemetry.sdk.TracklessConfig
   Trackless.configure(context = this, config = TracklessConfig(apiKey = "tl_your_api_key_here"))
   ```
3. Call typed event methods anywhere:
   - `Trackless.view("home")` — screen views (optional detail: `Trackless.view("settings", "notifications")`)
   - `Trackless.feature("export", "csv")` — feature usage; name the feature, put the variant in the optional detail
   - `Trackless.funnel("checkout", 0, "view_cart")` — multi-step flows
   - `Trackless.performance("api_fetch", durationSeconds = 0.342)` — timing in seconds (optional threshold: `Trackless.performance("api_fetch", durationSeconds = 0.342, thresholdSeconds = 2.0)`)
   - `Trackless.error("payment_failed", "DECLINED")` — something went wrong; counts toward errors per session and every alert
   - `Trackless.info("tier", "paid")` — something worth counting that the user did not do and that did not go wrong; never counts toward errors or alerts
   - `Trackless.startTimer("checkout")` / `Trackless.stopTimer("checkout", thresholdSeconds = 2.0)` / `Trackless.cancelTimer("checkout")`, or `Trackless.measure("csv_parse") { parser.parse(file) }` — timing without reading a clock yourself; session end discards open timers

## Important Rules

- Do NOT create an analytics wrapper class (`AnalyticsService`, `AnalyticsHelper`, a Hilt/Dagger-injected wrapper) — `Trackless` is already a thread-safe singleton object; call it directly
- `detail` is a SEPARATE parameter, never concatenated into the name: `Trackless.feature("export", "csv")`, not `Trackless.feature("export_csv")` — the dashboard groups detail distributions per name
- `funnel()` counts every call, in the same session or a later one — send a step from the code that completes it (the tap, the submitted form, the confirmed order), never from a composition, `LaunchedEffect`, `onResume` or a reload that can run more than once for one visit. Steps are counted separately; nothing links one to the next. When upgrading an existing integration to 0.7.0, move any funnel call that sits in such a path first: earlier versions counted it once per session
- Two levels only: `error(name, code?)` for what went wrong, `info(name, detail?)` for what did not. Call `info()` once per session for a property you want a session split on (`Trackless.info("tier", if (user.isPaid) "paid" else "free")`) — it counts sessions, not people. Report configuration many sessions share, never anything about the person. Never share a name between `error()` and `info()`
- The `severity` parameter on `error()` is deprecated but still compiles: `ErrorSeverity.ERROR`, `.WARNING` and `.FATAL` are sent as `error`, `.INFO` and `.DEBUG` as `info`
- The endpoint defaults to `https://api.tracklesstelemetry.com` — do NOT require the user to provide it
- Store the API key in `local.properties` + BuildConfig, not hardcoded in committed source
- Register the Application class in AndroidManifest.xml: `android:name=".MyApp"`
- Event names and fields (`detail`, `step`, `code`) are auto-normalized: lowercased, spaces/special chars replaced with `_`, trimmed, truncated to 100 chars. Developers can pass natural strings like `"Sign Up Button"` (becomes `"sign_up_button"`). Use the optional `detail` parameter on `feature()` and `view()` to distinguish variants — dashboard auto-groups features with detail values into donut charts
- Performance duration is in **seconds** (not milliseconds or nanoseconds)
- For launch time and main-thread hangs, use `TracklessConfig(..., measureLaunch = true, detectHangs = true)` instead of hand-rolled metrics. A process that is not in the foreground when `configure()` runs (started for a broadcast, job, service or provider, and opened later) sends no launch time, so call `configure()` in `Application.onCreate`; a launch longer than 60 seconds is dropped. Both are off by default; turning either on means the app must declare **App info and performance → Diagnostics** in its Play Data safety form — tell the developer
- To count uncaught exceptions, use `TracklessConfig(..., captureUncaught = true)`: each uncaught exception is sent as `error("trackless.uncaught", code = <class name>)`, with no message or stack trace, chained to any crash reporter already installed (Crashlytics keeps working). ANRs and native crashes are not counted. Off by default; turning it on means the app must declare **App info and performance → Crash logs** in its Play Data safety form — tell the developer. With it off, the SDK captures no crashes
- Names starting with `trackless.` are reserved for the SDK's own events (`trackless.launch`, `trackless.hang`, `trackless.uncaught`) — never pass one to `performance()`, `startTimer`, `measure`, `error()` or `info()`
- All methods are non-blocking, thread-safe, and never throw — `flush()` and `destroy()` send on the SDK's own flush thread, so both are safe on the main thread
- To change the API key at runtime (the user switches organization), call `configure()` again with the new key — the current session ends and is sent under the old key. Do NOT call `destroy()` first. A repeat call with identical settings is a no-op
- Environment auto-detects: FLAG_DEBUGGABLE → SANDBOX, otherwise PRODUCTION
- App version and build number are auto-read from PackageManager (the app's own versionName / versionCode)
- The SDK sends no distribution channel and reads no install date and no install source — do NOT add firstInstallTime, getInstallSourceInfo or getInstallerPackageName
- No GAID/SSAID — no permissions required
- Sessions are managed automatically via ActivityLifecycleCallbacks
- For Compose, use `LaunchedEffect` on `currentBackStackEntryAsState()` for view tracking
- No ProGuard/R8 configuration needed — the SDK uses no reflection or runtime class lookup, so nothing needs keeping. It ships no consumer rules file because none is required
